---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CodeCard from '../components/CodeCard.astro';

const REPO = 'https://github.com/nuthatch-org/nuthatch';
const RFC = REPO + '/blob/main/docs/rfcs/0053-graph-subgraph-compatibility.md#measured-outcome';
const WHAT_IT_IS = REPO + '/blob/main/docs/graph-compatibility-what-it-is.md';
const DIALECT = REPO + '/blob/main/docs/graph-query-dialect-accepted.md';
const SKILL = REPO + '/tree/main/skills/nuthatch-subgraph-port';
const BETSWIRL = REPO + '/blob/main/docs/stopgap/betswirl-bnb.md';

const port = `# 1. Report first. Nothing is written.
nuthatch port-report --dir path/to/subgraph > port-report.md

# 2. Scaffold from the manifest, then overlay views, calls and checks.
nuthatch init --from-subgraph <deployment CID> --dir nest
nuthatch port-emit --dir path/to/subgraph --out nest`;

const fromAddress = `nuthatch init 0xYourContract --chain arbitrum-one
nuthatch dev
nuthatch sql "SELECT count(*) FROM <alias>__<event>"`;

const answers: [string, string][] = [
  ['ids, timestamps, block numbers, log indices', 'prices, and anything derived from one'],
  ['addresses, token ids, transaction hashes', 'priced totals: volume in USD, fees, TVL'],
  ["values taken straight from an event's parameters", 'values from ordered, stateful mapping code'],
  ['sums and counts over events, where a view reproduces them', 'day and hour aggregate entities'],
  ['@derivedFrom relations between the above', ''],
];
---
<Layout
  path="/migrate"
  title="Move a subgraph to Nuthatch - Nuthatch"
  description="What carries over when a subgraph moves to a nest, and what does not. The Graph-dialect read surface is exact for event-shaped fields and refuses the rest by name; it is not a drop-in replacement. Or start from the contract address instead."
>
  <section class="page">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="section-label">Migrate</p>
      <h1>Moving a subgraph to a nest.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A nest can answer part of a subgraph's GraphQL, exactly, and refuse the rest by name. It is not a drop-in
        replacement, and most clients cannot adopt it by changing a URL. Before you move anything, find out which
        of your queries fall inside the part it answers. This page is how.
      </p>

      <section class="block">
        <p class="section-label">The boundary</p>
        <h2>Event-shaped fields answer. Mapping-computed ones do not.</h2>
        <p>
          A nest is a compiler over decoded chain events, not a second indexer. It does not run AssemblyScript.
          Whatever a subgraph takes straight from events, it reproduces exactly; whatever a mapping computes in order,
          carrying state from one event to the next, it refuses. Every field either answers exactly or is refused with
          a reason. There is no third behaviour: no default, no stale value, no empty list in place of an answer.
        </p>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table class="ctable">
            <thead>
              <tr><th scope="col" class="ct-us">Answers exactly</th><th scope="col">Refused by name</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {answers.map(([yes, no]) => (
                <tr><td class="ct-us">{yes}</td><td>{no}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="foot">
          From <a href={WHAT_IT_IS} rel="noopener">what the compatibility surface is, and what it is not</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="block">
        <p class="section-label">Measured</p>
        <h2>Why most clients cannot switch by changing a URL.</h2>
        <p>
          GraphQL has no partial answer. A query that names one field a nest cannot answer is refused whole, and the
          client gets an error rather than the other fields. So field coverage is an upper bound on query coverage,
          and a loose one.
        </p>
        <dl class="stats">
          <div><dt>70 <span>of 184</span></dt><dd>fields classed exact that a nest answered on the pinned Uniswap V4 port (38%)</dd></div>
          <div><dt>41 <span>of 93</span></dt><dd>on Carbon, the second pinned target (44%), which has no priced fields</dd></div>
          <div><dt>0 <span>of 4</span></dt><dd>fields answered in <code>pools(orderBy: totalValueLockedUSD)</code> with volume and token symbol</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <p>
          All three were measured on 12 September 2026 and are recorded in
          <a href={RFC} rel="noopener">RFC-0053's measured outcome</a>. For a Uniswap-shaped analytics client, the
          honest summary is that a nest answers nothing it would ask for. For a bot, a reconciliation job or an audit
          trail asking for every Swap on a pool since some block, with sender, transaction hash and log index, it
          answers exactly over the whole history. The shape of the subgraph decides the outcome far more than
          anything left to build. The programme is parked: what shipped is supported, and nothing further is being
          added to it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="routes">
        <section class="card route">
          <p class="section-label">Route one</p>
          <h2>Port the subgraph, and read the report first.</h2>
          <p>
            <code>port-report</code> classifies every entity field against the mappings and writes nothing.
            <code>init --from-subgraph</code> takes the manifest's data sources, ABIs and start blocks, and
            <code>port-emit</code> overlays the views and prints a coverage line naming every field it cannot answer.
            <code>port-emit</code> needs the subgraph's source, its <code>schema.graphql</code> and mappings, because
            a deployment CID holds only compiled WASM. Check your own queries' fields against that list.
          </p>
          <CodeCard code={port} lang="bash" label="shell" />
          <p>
            The default binary has no GraphQL route: <code>POST /graphql</code> answers 404. Serving the Graph dialect
            needs the separate <code>nuthatch-graph</code> download attached to every release since 4.11.0, described
            under <a href="/install#graph-build">the graph build</a>. The
            <a href={SKILL} rel="noopener">subgraph port skill</a> walks a coding agent through the same steps.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section class="card route">
          <p class="section-label">Route two</p>
          <h2>Start from the contract address instead.</h2>
          <p>
            If you own the client, it is often simpler to leave GraphQL behind. Give <code>init</code> the contracts
            the subgraph indexed and it makes one table per event from the ABI, with no mappings and no schema to
            write. Then ask in SQL, over HTTP at <code>/sql</code> or from an agent over MCP. Counts, sums and joins
            over events are a view in <code>views/*.sql</code>, or a maintained entity when it must be fast.
          </p>
          <CodeCard code={fromAddress} lang="bash" label="shell" />
          <p>
            Prices are the same problem by this route: nothing derives a USD value for you. The
            <a href="/example">worked example</a> goes from address to view to entity, and
            <a href="/agents">the agents page</a> connects the result to Claude Code or Cursor.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>

      <div class="scope">
        <section>
          <p class="cap cap-in">What carries over</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Data sources, ABIs and start blocks, read from the manifest by <code>init --from-subgraph</code>.</li>
            <li>Factory templates, where a parameter names the template; the rest are listed for you to resolve.</li>
            <li>Introspection, filters, ordering, pagination and <code>_meta</code>, in the graph build.</li>
            <li>Event-shaped fields, exactly, over the hot store and sealed history alike.</li>
            <li>Queries at <code>/graphql</code> and <code>/subgraphs/id/&lt;deployment&gt;</code>.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
        <section>
          <p class="cap cap-out">What does not</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Mapping code. AssemblyScript is never run, in indexing or anywhere else.</li>
            <li>Prices and priced totals such as <code>derivedETH</code> and <code>totalValueLockedUSD</code>. By design, not by backlog.</li>
            <li>Day and hour aggregate entities, which have no table.</li>
            <li>A query that names any refused field: it fails whole.</li>
            <li>The GraphQL route in the default binary, and any shape not on <a href={DIALECT} rel="noopener">the accepted dialect</a>.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>
      </div>

      <div class="notes">
        <section>
          <h2>We can host the port</h2>
          <p>
            We port a subgraph that is no longer live on the network to a nest and run it on our servers, with no
            billing and no accounts. Its event-derived fields answer exactly and the rest are refused by name, so check
            your app's queries against the port before switching. <a href="/subgraphs">The hosting offer</a> has the
            terms, and the <a href={BETSWIRL} rel="noopener">BetSwirl BNB Chain handback</a> shows one port end to end:
            its client's own queries answered exactly.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section>
          <h2>Beside the network, not instead of it</h2>
          <p>
            A subgraph that the network still serves is usually best left there. A nest is for the case where a
            subgraph has stopped, or where you would rather hold your own contracts' history on your own machine.
            <a href="/the-graph">Nuthatch and The Graph</a> sets out how the two sit together, and
            <a href="/compare">the comparison</a> puts Nuthatch beside The Graph, Ponder, rindexer and Envio.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .page { padding-block: clamp(3rem, 6vw, 4.5rem) clamp(4rem, 9vw, 6.5rem); }
  .page section { padding-block: 0; }
  h1 { margin-bottom: 0.8rem; }
  .lede {
    color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 1.08rem; line-height: 1.7;
    margin-bottom: 1rem; max-width: 62ch;
  }
  .block { margin-top: 3.5rem; }
  .block h2, .route h2 { font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 2.6vw, 1.6rem); margin-bottom: 0.9rem; max-width: 30ch; }
  .block p, .route p, .notes p { color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.97rem; line-height: 1.68; margin: 0 0 1rem; max-width: 72ch; }

  .table-scroll { overflow-x: auto; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius); margin: 1.4rem 0 0.8rem; }
  .ctable { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; min-width: 32rem; font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .ctable th, .ctable td { text-align: left; padding: 0.8rem 1rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); vertical-align: top; width: 50%; color: var(--text); }
  .ctable thead th { font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace; font-size: 0.78rem; color: var(--text-muted); font-weight: 500; }
  .ctable tbody tr:last-child td { border-bottom: 0; }
  .ct-us { background: var(--pale-blue-bg); }
  thead .ct-us { color: var(--accent-ink); font-weight: 600; }
  .block p.foot { font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--text-muted); }

  .stats {
    margin: 1.6rem 0; display: grid; gap: 1px;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(14rem, 100%), 1fr));
    background: var(--border); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius); overflow: hidden;
  }
  .stats div { background: var(--bg-elev); padding: 1.2rem 1.3rem; }
  .stats dt { font-size: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 2.6rem); font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: -0.03em; line-height: 1.1; }
  .stats dt span { font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace; font-size: 0.85rem; letter-spacing: 0.02em; color: var(--rust); font-weight: 500; }
  .stats dd { margin: 0.5rem 0 0; color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.88rem; line-height: 1.55; }

  .routes {
    margin-top: 3.5rem; display: grid; gap: 1.2rem;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(24rem, 100%), 1fr));
  }
  .route { min-width: 0; }
  .route .section-label { color: var(--rust); }

  .scope {
    margin-top: 3rem; display: grid; gap: 1.2rem;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(20rem, 100%), 1fr));
  }
  .scope section { border-top: 1px solid var(--border); padding-top: 1rem; }
  .cap {
    font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace; font-size: 0.7rem; letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase; margin-bottom: 0.7rem;
  }
  .cap-in { color: var(--ok); }
  .cap-out { color: var(--rust); }
  .scope ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.1rem; display: grid; gap: 0.5rem; }
  .scope li { color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.93rem; line-height: 1.6; }
  .scope li::marker { color: var(--rust); }

  .notes {
    margin-top: 3.5rem; display: grid; gap: 2rem;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(20rem, 100%), 1fr));
  }
  .notes h2 { font-size: clamp(1.15rem, 2.2vw, 1.35rem); margin-bottom: 0.6rem; }
</style>
